    summary: 'Why anxiety is common in a pregnancy that follows a loss, how to be supported and which signs should lead you to ask for help.',
    body_md: `## In brief
- After a miscarriage, a new pregnancy can bring **joy and fear** together. This ambivalence is common and says nothing about your ability to be a parent.
- A **pregnancy that follows a painful one**, or that comes after a period of infertility, is among the situations where emotions are more intense, according to the Assurance Maladie (the French national health insurance).
- Talk about your worries **at every appointment**: the doctor or sage-femme (midwife) can adapt the support.
- The **early prenatal interview** (entretien prénatal précoce), offered in the 4th month, and the **birth preparation** sessions are times to talk about it.

## Understanding the issue
When a previous pregnancy has ended, it is hard to look ahead. Some women and partners describe holding their breath until the stage at which the loss had happened, being on guard every moment at the slightest sensation, or finding it hard to announce the pregnancy and to become attached. Santé.fr (a French public health information site) reports the account of an expectant mother so anxious at the idea of going through another miscarriage that she thought of nothing else, and who found support among relatives who had been through the same thing.

These emotions are understandable. They deserve to be welcomed, because anxiety can become exhausting. The Assurance Maladie estimates that anxiety affects 5 to 15% of pregnant women and depression about 10%. It is advised to see a doctor when anxiety becomes intolerable, when fears are constant or obsessive, in the event of panic attacks, constant irritability, a very frequent need to be reassured, or sleep or eating problems.

**A reassuring marker**, according to the Assurance Maladie: miscarriage is most often an isolated event and subsequent pregnancies generally go without difficulty. Likewise, about a quarter of women have bleeding in the first trimester and continue their pregnancy; isolated bleeding does not always herald a miscarriage.

## Day to day
- **Say so at the first appointment**: "I had a miscarriage, I am very worried." This lets the professional support you better.
- **Ask what is planned**: dates of ultrasound scans, who to contact in case of doubt, what to do in the event of bleeding.
- **Find what soothes you**: walking, nature, breathing, music, time together or alone. Lighten your obligations when possible.
- **Choose who to tell about the pregnancy, and when**, without feeling obliged to follow a timetable.
- **Limit online searches** if they increase the anxiety; instead, note down your questions for the appointment.
- **Think about psychological support**: a psychologist, a PMI centre (France's free mother-and-child health service), the Mon soutien psy scheme (sessions with an approved psychologist covered by the Assurance Maladie, depending on the scheme's conditions), support groups.
- **Involve your partner**: they may carry the same fear, or a different one. Also let them know the signs that should lead you to ask for help.

## Frequently asked questions
**Can my stress cause a miscarriage?**
An isolated early miscarriage is most often due to an abnormality in the development of the embryo. Taking care of your stress is important for you, but you are not responsible for what happened.

**I cannot manage to be happy: will I love my baby less well?**
Santé.fr reminds us that discomfort or doubts during pregnancy do not predict a bad parent. Attachment is built gradually, often over the course of the pregnancy and after the birth.

**Can I ask for extra tests to be reassured?**
Talk about it with your doctor or your sage-femme. They will tell you what is medically useful and can offer support suited to your worry.

## When to seek advice
Call **15** (SAMU, France's emergency medical service) or **112** in the event of heavy bleeding, feeling faint, dizziness, fever or intense abdominal pain; see a doctor the same day in the event of moderate bleeding or pain similar to period pain. Speak quickly to a professional if anxiety stops you from sleeping, eating or living your daily life, or if great sadness sets in. In case of distress or dark thoughts, call **3114** (France's national suicide prevention helpline), at any hour.

This article provides general information and does not replace the advice of a health professional (doctor, midwife, pharmacist).`,
